{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1750 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "9f4296ff759ef1230827c5b30250e3a9ebda449329b39ec6e9189ceb4e074ea4" :edb/db-before-t 1749 :edb/db-after-t 1750 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535062 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:58:12.210Z" 13194139535062 true] [17592186087404 1100 "Find the point where the line $y = -x + 3$ intersects the circle $(x+3)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 8$." 13194139535062 false] [17592186087404 1100 "Find the point where the line $y = -x + 3$ intersects the circle $(x+3)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 8.$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186087404 1145 "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute\n\n$$\ny = -x + 3\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)^{2} + (y - 2)^{2} &= 8 \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} + ((-x + 3) - 2)^{2} &= 8 \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} + (-x + 1)^{2} &= 8 \\\\\nx^{2} + 6x + 9 + x^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 8 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 4x + 10 &= 8 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x + 5 &= 4 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx + 1 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to plug in $x = -1$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -x + 3 \\\\\n&= - (-1) + 3 \\\\\n&= 1 + 3 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line\n\n$$\ny = -x + 3\n$$\n\nintersects the circle\n\n$$\n(x+3)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 8\n$$\n\nat the point $(-1,4)$.\n\nA sketch of the circle and the line is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/845/Source/Images/81b24b7386f2be0f994996e137e4dae6.png)" 13194139535062 false] [17592186087404 1145 "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y = -x + 3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+((-x+3)-2)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(-x+1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9+{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x+10 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+5 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to plug in $x = -1$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x+3 \\\\  & =-(-1)+3 \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y = -x + 3$ intersects the circle $(x+3)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 8$ at the point $(-1,4).$\n\nA sketch of the circle and the line is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/90f7e5f187ad79e18d36ce253c8f1f998bc5a92790909f4840c84789c2ae5222.png)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186087405 1100 "Find the points where the circle $(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 3$ intersects the line $y = 3x - 1$." 13194139535062 false] [17592186087405 1100 "Find the points where the circle $(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 3$ intersects the line $y = 3x - 1.$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186087405 1145 "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y = 3x - 1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 2)^{2} + (y + 1)^{2} &= 3 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} + ((3x - 1) + 1)^{2} &= 3 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} + (3x)^{2} &= 3 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + 4 + 9x^{2} &= 3 \\\\\n10x^{2} - 4x + 1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= (-4)^{2} - 4 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 16 - 40 \\\\\n&=-24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = -24 < 0\n$$\n\n, there are no real solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n10x^2 - 4x + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle\n\n$$\n(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 3\n$$\n\ndoes not intercept the line $y = 3x - 1$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/845/Source/Images/36b7751ad0069cf451aaf83b66d1a3ab.png)" 13194139535062 false] [17592186087405 1145 "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y = 3x - 1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+((3x-1)+1)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(3x)^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+9{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 10\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{D} = -24 < 0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $10x^2 - 4x + 1 = 0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 3$ does not intercept the line $y = 3x - 1$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/590fa0a3fb8cb0e80c46859ad19b9457528c40f79f1ba33edea217d8500b64de.png)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186087406 1100 "The line $-4x+3y=24$ intersects the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter, find the equation of the circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/c8ed2363f6899e90eff46d8e45107ed5eee19582aeaed624c9831e5a128ff10a.png)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186087406 1100 "The line $-4x+3y=24$ intersects the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter, find the equation of the circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/845/Source/Images/9d915606f37edf8b96db4b680ba3cecc.png)" 13194139535062 false] [17592186087406 1145 "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ and solve\n\n$$\n-4x+3y=24\n$$\n\nfor $x$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 3(0) &= 24 \\\\\n- 4x &= 24 \\\\\nx &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4(0) + 3y &= 24 \\\\\n3y &= 24 \\\\\ny &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-6,0)$ and $(0,8)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{-6+0}{2}, \\dfrac{0+8}{2}\\right) = (-3,4)\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(-3,4)$ and a point on the circle, say $B(0,8)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{(0 - (-3))^{2} + (8 - 4)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(-3,4)$ and radius $r=5$, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - (-3))^{2} + (y - 4)^{2} &= 5^{2} \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} + (y - 4)^{2} &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535062 false] [17592186087406 1145 "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ and solve $-4x+3y=24$ for $x$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3(0) & =24 \\\\ -4x & =24 \\\\ x & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(0)+3y & =24 \\\\ 3y & =24 \\\\ y & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-6,0)$ and $(0,8)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{-6+0}{2}, \\dfrac{0+8}{2}\\right) = (-3,4).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(-3,4)$ and a point on the circle, say $B(0,8),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{(0-(-3))^{2}+(8-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(-3,4)$ and radius $r=5,$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-3))^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186087549 1100 "Find the points where the line $y = x +5$ intersects the circle $(x+2)^2 + y^2 = 9$." 13194139535062 false] [17592186087549 1100 "Find the points where the line $y = x +5$ intersects the circle $(x+2)^2 + y^2 = 9.$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186087549 1145 "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection points, we need to substitute\n\n$$\ny = x + 5\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 2)^{2} + y^{2} &= 9 \\\\\n(x + 2)^{2} + (x + 5)^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nx^{2} + 4x + 4 + x^{2} + 10x + 25 &= 9 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 14x + 29 &= 9 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 14x + 20 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 7x + 10 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 2)(x + 5) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -2,-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute each of $x = -2$ and $x=-5$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$, we get\n$y|= x + 5; =-2 + 5; = 3$.\nThis corresponds to an intersection point $(-2,3)$.\n- Substituting $x=-5$, we get\n$y|= x + 5; =-5 + 5; = 0$.\nThis corresponds to an intersection point $(-5,0)$.\n\nTherefore, the line\n\n$$\ny = x + 5\n$$\n\nintersects the circle\n\n$$\n(x+2)^2 + y^2 = 9\n$$\n\nat the points $(-2,3)$ and $(-5,0)$.\n\nA sketch of the circle and the line is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/845/Source/Images/35167f849b7ed3149fb0e1beed852a8f.png)" 13194139535062 false] [17592186087549 1145 "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y = x + 5$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(x+5)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+{x}^{2}+10x+25 & =9 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+14x+29 & =9 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+14x+20 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute each of $x = -2$ and $x=-5$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+5 \\\\  & =-2+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to an intersection point $(-2,3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+5 \\\\  & =-5+5 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to an intersection point $(-5,0).$\n\nTherefore, the line $y = x + 5$ intersects the circle $(x+2)^2 + y^2 = 9$ at the points $(-2,3)$ and $(-5,0).$\n\nA sketch of the circle and the line is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/9298e1af177eb9ba1702af0053c03d1d0e696819bae717c4892efdcd574c78fc.png)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186087598 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329062 13194139535062 true] [17592186087598 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329069 13194139535062 true] [17592186087598 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329132 13194139535062 true] [17592186087598 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329160 13194139535062 true] [17592186087599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329076 13194139535062 true] [17592186087599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329083 13194139535062 true] [17592186087599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329139 13194139535062 true] [17592186087599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329167 13194139535062 true] [17592186087599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329174 13194139535062 true] [17592186087600 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329090 13194139535062 true] [17592186087600 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329097 13194139535062 true] [17592186087600 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329104 13194139535062 true] [17592186087600 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329118 13194139535062 true] [17592186087600 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329181 13194139535062 true] [17592186087723 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329055 13194139535062 true] [17592186087723 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329111 13194139535062 true] [17592186087723 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329125 13194139535062 true] [17592186087723 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329146 13194139535062 true] [17592186087723 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329153 13194139535062 true] [17592186088263 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535062 true] [17592186088263 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/f17e4718742158f65db70bd25e0ba97fd0fba793b5212eee0ee60072eb4231e9.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y=x$ intersects the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=8.$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186088263 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/845/Source/Images/q-7585.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y = x$ intersects the circle $(x + 4)^{2} + y^{2} = 8$." 13194139535062 false] [17592186088263 1145 "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=x$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+16+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+2 & =0 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=-2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\ny=x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=x$ intersects the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=8$ at the point $(-2,-2).$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329055 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329056 13194139535062 true] [17592186329055 1089 "q-117247" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329055 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535062 true] [17592186329055 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/5180dfe04f31f43fdcdfa8bd720951df0c6fb0d73cd2db3912b37e172f714131.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ intersects the line $x-3y=-4$ (sketched above)." 13194139535062 true] [17592186329055 1113 #uuid "f9516424-4bd5-5885-82ea-4588d28a4cdd" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329055 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=-4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{8x}{9}+\\frac{16}{9} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{10x}{9}+\\frac{25}{9} & =\\frac{45}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{10x}{9}-\\frac{20}{9} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-1,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-1,1).$ Next, we plug in $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=-4$ intersects the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ at the points $(2,2)$ and $(-1,1).$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329056 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329057 13194139535062 true] [17592186329056 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329058 13194139535062 true] [17592186329056 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329059 13194139535062 true] [17592186329056 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329060 13194139535062 true] [17592186329056 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329061 13194139535062 true] [17592186329056 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329056 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329056 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329060 13194139535062 true] [17592186329056 1110 #uuid "5ececc18-1ac1-5cc4-865d-259fc6da1ed1" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329057 1034 "$(2,2)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6})$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329057 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329057 1111 #uuid "9ee5a54a-bfba-5cfa-b0a4-267b6ecd3c54" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329058 1034 "$(3,\\frac{7}{3})$ and $(-1,1)$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329058 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329058 1111 #uuid "85826fff-b2b3-5574-871e-fcc02e875021" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329059 1034 "$(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6})$ and $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4})$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329059 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329059 1111 #uuid "549f7dc4-8b52-5bca-9629-1e67e6233db3" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329060 1034 "$(2,2)$ and $(-1,1)$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329060 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329060 1111 #uuid "d90d92ef-71e0-5265-aa9c-ceff1cb328d2" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329061 1034 "$(3,\\frac{7}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6})$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329061 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329061 1111 #uuid "d3a566e7-ffa5-5ec6-9fc0-2a252b40996d" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329062 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329063 13194139535062 true] [17592186329062 1089 "q-117542" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329062 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535062 true] [17592186329062 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/a3a9751a19c9a853c425b81ce63ccb76b4cd4350f152df3dbe00a1cdb0f8cdab.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y=-3x-11$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10.$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329062 1113 #uuid "67a9b2cd-1787-5032-a8c9-13cecd77788f" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329062 1145 "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=-3x-11$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-3x-11+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-3x-10)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}+60x+100 & =10 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+60x+100 & =10 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+60x+90 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=-3$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-11 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-11 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=-3x-11$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10$ at the point $(-3,-2).$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329064 13194139535062 true] [17592186329063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329065 13194139535062 true] [17592186329063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329066 13194139535062 true] [17592186329063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329067 13194139535062 true] [17592186329063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329068 13194139535062 true] [17592186329063 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329063 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329063 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329064 13194139535062 true] [17592186329063 1110 #uuid "9f96ad68-0d47-56ff-a4ec-bb8b1a61ea5d" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329064 1034 "(-3,-2)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329064 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329064 1111 #uuid "6b73fd24-5d1d-549f-ab0b-97f073f6002e" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329065 1034 "(-2,-5)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329065 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329065 1111 #uuid "7dfc237b-0391-53f4-9e07-18741e9ef4be" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329066 1034 "(-1.5,-6.5)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329066 1111 #uuid "92277082-f9fa-5699-9813-eeb338f3ff98" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329067 1034 "(-2.5,-3.5)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329067 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329067 1111 #uuid "092f5a5c-6a4c-5ad7-b107-25c85dafb8db" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329068 1034 "(-4,1)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329068 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329068 1111 #uuid "f1be655d-1d80-5110-9c7d-43bb4301d17d" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329069 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329070 13194139535062 true] [17592186329069 1089 "q-117546" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329069 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535062 true] [17592186329069 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/91ead412d3e333fd4bfbcce785813a530e66ca39858ca108e9920d349443bb53.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point where the line $-2x+3y=12$ intersects the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=13$ (sketched above)." 13194139535062 true] [17592186329069 1113 #uuid "90e9bd05-dbac-514e-ba2a-1c98522457da" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329069 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation $-2x+3y=12,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2x}{3}+4\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{2x}{3}+4$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+4+1)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+5)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{3}+25 & =13 \\\\ \\frac{13{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{26x}{3}+13 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329071 13194139535062 true] [17592186329070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329072 13194139535062 true] [17592186329070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329073 13194139535062 true] [17592186329070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329074 13194139535062 true] [17592186329070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329075 13194139535062 true] [17592186329070 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329070 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329070 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329073 13194139535062 true] [17592186329070 1110 #uuid "5a61f814-3085-5e3e-982f-b82601f07526" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329071 1034 "-5" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329071 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329071 1111 #uuid "73641b57-7ccc-5781-bbb1-059986ebf422" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329072 1034 "-4" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329072 1111 #uuid "2a9df10d-0406-5afa-8406-2bfc116d2091" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329073 1034 "-3" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329073 1111 #uuid "3803a30f-e30d-5793-bbc5-96e08b0ae9df" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329074 1034 "-1" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329074 1111 #uuid "6307667c-c334-55b8-8947-075a4896981c" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329075 1034 "-2" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329075 1111 #uuid "c24e1bac-3c95-597a-9c23-651cb8ce5d6d" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329076 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329077 13194139535062 true] [17592186329076 1089 "q-117734" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329076 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535062 true] [17592186329076 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1$ intersects the line $y=-3x.$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329076 1113 #uuid "ea101bc1-4f0a-50d4-b42a-0a6309938246" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329076 1145 "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-3x$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(-3x+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+9{x}^{2}-6x+1 & =1 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 10\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-24<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $10{x}^{2}-4x+1=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1$ does not intersect the line $y=-3x.$ \n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/87f75953dbe795a8e5db201778b4ff3127f02bae30ef3b8e8ffa7bd7956c1bc3.png)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329078 13194139535062 true] [17592186329077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329079 13194139535062 true] [17592186329077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329080 13194139535062 true] [17592186329077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329081 13194139535062 true] [17592186329077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329082 13194139535062 true] [17592186329077 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329077 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329077 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329081 13194139535062 true] [17592186329077 1110 #uuid "13942aed-5583-5808-af56-0712bdc58712" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329078 1034 "$-3$ and $3$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329078 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329078 1111 #uuid "82379688-2b6e-5b92-8d47-74542f278bd7" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329079 1034 "$0$ only" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329079 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329079 1111 #uuid "f55058c9-58fd-5b86-8bfa-1216f657b136" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329080 1034 "$-1$ and $1$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329080 1111 #uuid "457da568-a1b7-5aef-bc5c-199e6b40d3a6" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329081 1034 "The circle and the line do not intersect" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329081 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329081 1111 #uuid "fd682f0b-f0a5-5698-831b-22adcc338263" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329082 1034 "$-2$ and $2$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329082 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329082 1111 #uuid "933645e4-7426-5e55-85c4-26bb808dab00" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329083 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329084 13194139535062 true] [17592186329083 1089 "q-117737" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329083 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535062 true] [17592186329083 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ intersects the line $x+2y=4$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329083 1113 #uuid "61ffdc01-8414-55ad-b1b4-42608bbe9957" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329083 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation $x+2y=4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2}x+2)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+4 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={2}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4-15 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-11<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ does not intersect the line $x+2y=4$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/400b997f9f1e83d6d2dd6c34b68a01ad4de5b352e40de2401eb516ca181f3163.png)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329084 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329085 13194139535062 true] [17592186329084 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329086 13194139535062 true] [17592186329084 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329087 13194139535062 true] [17592186329084 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329088 13194139535062 true] [17592186329084 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329089 13194139535062 true] [17592186329084 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329084 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329084 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329088 13194139535062 true] [17592186329084 1110 #uuid "2963a972-c781-52d2-a755-4aa69c211d09" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329085 1034 "$1$ only" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329085 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329085 1111 #uuid "aaddf264-1d25-5a7b-b2be-f5563dc7bad2" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329086 1034 "$2$ only" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329086 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329086 1111 #uuid "1d1a878c-7f2f-5c35-bf28-7b0cf8af73a1" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329087 1034 "$-1$ and $3$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329087 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329087 1111 #uuid "10fefbb7-794d-58e2-a2c3-611ff4449143" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329088 1034 "The circle and the line do not intersect" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329088 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329088 1111 #uuid "65de35fb-0f08-5436-9a3c-c6f3c99f492f" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329089 1034 "$-2$ and $4$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329089 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329089 1111 #uuid "1d4549cb-c476-521e-9ec0-1fdcd890b7eb" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329090 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329091 13194139535062 true] [17592186329090 1089 "q-117851" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329090 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535062 true] [17592186329090 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/0e5d6c9ce3198b5950c8a5396cf5500f4bddd232fbe44613611873c294dda6ea.png)\n\nThe line $3y+x=12$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." 13194139535062 true] [17592186329090 1113 #uuid "2c7a8305-48d6-5002-af81-74f86e2adf20" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329090 1145 "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+x & =12 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-0 & =12 \\\\ 3y & =12 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(12,0)$ and $(0,4)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{12+0}{2},\\frac{0+4}{2})=(6,2).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(6,2)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(12,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(6-12)}^{2}+(2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-6)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(6,2)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{10},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-6)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{10})}^{2} \\\\ (x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329091 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329092 13194139535062 true] [17592186329091 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329093 13194139535062 true] [17592186329091 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329094 13194139535062 true] [17592186329091 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329095 13194139535062 true] [17592186329091 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329096 13194139535062 true] [17592186329091 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329091 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329091 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329092 13194139535062 true] [17592186329091 1110 #uuid "50bc64f8-b568-5737-bf3a-4901e1a75286" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329092 1034 "(x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=40" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329092 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329092 1111 #uuid "165c41b