0)$ and $(0,-2)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{-8+0}{2},\\frac{0-2}{2})=(-4,-1).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(-4,-1)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(-8,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(-4+8)}^{2}+(-1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(-4,-1)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{17},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-(-4))}^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{17})}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329098 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329099 13194139535062 true] [17592186329098 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329100 13194139535062 true] [17592186329098 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329101 13194139535062 true] [17592186329098 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329102 13194139535062 true] [17592186329098 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329103 13194139535062 true] [17592186329098 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329098 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329098 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329101 13194139535062 true] [17592186329098 1110 #uuid "c7802fa3-d135-5961-8d50-b8a9a452ee00" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329099 1034 "(x+8)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=17" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329099 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329099 1111 #uuid "59548f33-3e11-584e-ad31-7a7410835c2a" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329100 1034 "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=68" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329100 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329100 1111 #uuid "36d03c1e-0297-5df9-be70-7a899b50d645" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329101 1034 "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=17" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329101 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329101 1111 #uuid "f34bb61f-428d-52ba-87f5-f2806044c96c" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329102 1034 "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=2\\sqrt{17}" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329102 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329102 1111 #uuid "d5755761-db97-5922-a440-b069e501e002" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329103 1034 "(x+8)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=68" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329103 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329103 1111 #uuid "ec1b8649-14f1-5ce5-821e-a2e725b06541" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329104 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329105 13194139535062 true] [17592186329104 1089 "q-117859" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329104 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535062 true] [17592186329104 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/c100b27df3e4d9b23846961ad5d168fbe6f0eecd94ab8a9b99f51e82bca064a3.png)\n\nThe line $2y-3x=-24$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." 13194139535062 true] [17592186329104 1113 #uuid "ac7dcd31-21b4-5064-a3d7-5c55f9b204d5" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329104 1145 "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-3x & =-24 \\\\ -3x & =-24 \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3(0) & =-24 \\\\ 2y & =-24 \\\\ y & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(8,0)$ and $(0,-12)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{8+0}{2},\\frac{0-12}{2})=(4,-6).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(4,-6)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(8,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(4-8)}^{2}+(-6-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(4,-6)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{13},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-6))^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{13})}^{2} \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2} & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329106 13194139535062 true] [17592186329105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329107 13194139535062 true] [17592186329105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329108 13194139535062 true] [17592186329105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329109 13194139535062 true] [17592186329105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329110 13194139535062 true] [17592186329105 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329105 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329105 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329107 13194139535062 true] [17592186329105 1110 #uuid "6ed40211-7a4a-5fdc-89f4-a0a73aa76dd0" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329106 1034 "(x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=13" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329106 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329106 1111 #uuid "67763a38-60d4-5ce2-bfb1-77d3fba66510" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329107 1034 "(x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=52" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329107 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329107 1111 #uuid "0bfaf101-903d-5e7f-9c9b-af5acb098a09" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329108 1034 "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=2\\sqrt{13}" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329108 1111 #uuid "e4b7a98a-d153-5eb2-bd71-ca1abaeb61a7" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329109 1034 "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=52" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329109 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329109 1111 #uuid "222bf662-e011-58d2-9a31-4c8b4450328f" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329110 1034 "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=13" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329110 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329110 1111 #uuid "5e2a6057-7c59-52a6-845f-800695f604fc" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329111 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329112 13194139535062 true] [17592186329111 1089 "q-14665" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329111 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535062 true] [17592186329111 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/8c9f433d2e23db9dc8abb81395e91acf59047e26221b1218393c4905548d1f35.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2}=34$ intersects the line $y=x+4$ (sketched above)." 13194139535062 true] [17592186329111 1113 #uuid "249a4827-1fab-5d89-acce-30cbdc7d02ff" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329111 1145 "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=x+4$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} & =34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(x+4-5)^{2} & =34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(x-1)^{2} & =34 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =34 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x-24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-2,6$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\  & =-2+4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-2,2).$ Next, we plug in $x=6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\  & =6+4 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(6,10).$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=x+4$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2}=34$ at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(6,10).$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329112 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329113 13194139535062 true] [17592186329112 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329114 13194139535062 true] [17592186329112 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329115 13194139535062 true] [17592186329112 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329116 13194139535062 t