194139535062 true] [17592186329118 1113 #uuid "83e94835-de37-5d69-b0fd-aa884e963d7d" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329118 1145 "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-4 & =y \\\\ y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(2,0)$ and $(0,-4)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2+0}{2},\\frac{0-4}{2})=(1,-2).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(1,-2)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(2,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(1-2)}^{2}+(-2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1)}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(1,-2)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{5},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329119 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329120 13194139535062 true] [17592186329119 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329121 13194139535062 true] [17592186329119 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329122 13194139535062 true] [17592186329119 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329123 13194139535062 true] [17592186329119 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329124 13194139535062 true] [17592186329119 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329119 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329119 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329124 13194139535062 true] [17592186329119 1110 #uuid "a9d5e498-4274-57b8-a27d-0a83df00dfdd" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329120 1034 "(x-1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329120 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329120 1111 #uuid "eada94d4-ae4c-52ab-a34c-861691c9edd2" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329121 1034 "(x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329121 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329121 1111 #uuid "23d3947b-a3b6-5250-b92b-c40bc7923269" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329122 1034 "(x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=5" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329122 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329122 1111 #uuid "30b95d83-3aa6-5d0b-a249-b1e0a9c2f7b9" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329123 1034 "(x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=10" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329123 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329123 1111 #uuid "4dc01482-028d-58f2-a3fa-ec8e6afea8f5" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329124 1034 "(x-1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=5" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329124 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329124 1111 #uuid "5c757116-1504-5ffc-94d0-49f889371b78" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329125 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329126 13194139535062 true] [17592186329125 1089 "q-15805" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329125 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535062 true] [17592186329125 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/de12c0b5be8318cc21c62615d24125998c22d55d37abafdee37c103c3840cb71.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ intersects the line $x-3y=2$ (sketched above)." 13194139535062 true] [17592186329125 1113 #uuid "cfa05974-d33e-5f3c-90f3-bf0b329bfc3f" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329125 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4)}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{10}{3})}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+10 & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-4,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-4,-2).$ Next, we plug in $x=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=2$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ at the points $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0).$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329127 13194139535062 true] [17592186329126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329128 13194139535062 true] [17592186329126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329129 13194139535062 true] [17592186329126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329130 13194139535062 true] [17592186329126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329131 13194139535062 true] [17592186329126 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329126 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329126 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329128 13194139535062 true] [17592186329126 1110 #uuid "5fddc856-8795-517d-97c8-053c51c2231b" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329127 1034 "$(-4,-2)$ and $(1,0)$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329127 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329127 1111 #uuid "7186e710-e7d2-5f97-91b8-fc81553ade1d" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329128 1034 "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0)$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329128 1111 #uuid "74f35b7a-e710-5552-8eff-fdbd9fe09c8f" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329129 1034 "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,0)$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329129 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329129 1111 #uuid "2ca4f91f-7eb1-5ef4-8200-3295055974eb" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329130 1034 "$(-3,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329130 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329130 1111 #uuid "ed6f3bfb-f40a-563e-8267-c86f1e878913" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329131 1034 "$(-4,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329131 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329131 1111 #uuid "a1099d2b-058c-5dfe-8647-be9cabb1413e" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329132 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329133 13194139535062 true] [17592186329132 1089 "q-15806" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329132 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535062 true] [17592186329132 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/43fe48b1980d01aa9dbb7cc0f614272ad19e7cfe47b5a5a83a0f81e7539f285b.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=10$ intersects the line $3y-x=-7.$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329132 1113 #uuid "df3cd888-e784-5638-9690-b7ff43d4a76a" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329132 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation $3y-x=-7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}+5)}^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{16x}{9}+\\frac{64}{9} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+10 & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=1$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $3y-x=-7$ intersects the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=10$ at the point $(1,-2).$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329134 13194139535062 true] [17592186329133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329135 13194139535062 true] [17592186329133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329136 13194139535062 true] [17592186329133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329137 13194139535062 true] [17592186329133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329138 13194139535062 true] [17592186329133 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329133 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329133 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329134 13194139535062 true] [17592186329133 1110 #uuid "b14fa81e-6252-56bd-ba2d-872f75be5089" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329134 1034 "(1,-2)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329134 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329134 1111 #uuid "9c968461-bf24-53a6-854e-2ed083bb1c8b" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329135 1034 "(2,-4)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329135 1111 #uuid "8494e28e-c816-530d-a46e-8c2f51dd4277" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329136 1034 "(1,-3)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329136 1111 #uuid "7610e9d2-4bfd-5e3b-8abf-8f5efebb4349" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329137 1034 "(3,-5)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329137 1111 #uuid "37f9290c-5e0b-565b-b93d-d8acf994d262" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329138 1034 "(2,-3)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329138 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329138 1111 #uuid "252af879-fed6-5806-95fd-54362544d1fa" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329139 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329140 13194139535062 true] [17592186329139 1089 "q-54242" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329139 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535062 true] [17592186329139 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ intersects the line $y=-x+3.$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329139 1113 #uuid "6de12d86-c297-540f-8fcc-949f18bd056f" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329139 1145 "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-x+3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(-x+3)^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+{x}^{2}-6x+9 & =5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-24<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $2{x}^{2}-4x+5=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ does not intersect the line $y=-x+3$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/85c9bea054f2e246d62864b022e53a6f1d23886b0f0c9f8c80c8e0a5b1be9cd8.png)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329141 13194139535062 true] [17592186329140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329142 13194139535062 true] [17592186329140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329143 13194139535062 true] [17592186329140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329144 13194139535062 true] [17592186329140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329145 13194139535062 true] [17592186329140 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329140 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329140 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329143 13194139535062 true] [17592186329140 1110 #uuid "20a20482-4a16-5f59-941c-b41061fd7257" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329141 1034 "$-3$ and $-1$" 13194139535062 true] [1