mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/80646a56ea6df17b6f0b6460f3d4918174966773b9e7910d252c9098af3b0f48.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10$ intersects the line $y=x-3$ (sketched above)." 13194139535062 true] [17592186329153 1113 #uuid "bd4f4650-cbfe-54ae-b334-1592fbad61b9" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329153 1145 "To find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we substitute $y=x-3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(x-3+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(x-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16+{x}^{2}-4x+4 & =10 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-12x+10 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329155 13194139535062 true] [17592186329154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329156 13194139535062 true] [17592186329154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329157 13194139535062 true] [17592186329154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329158 13194139535062 true] [17592186329154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329159 13194139535062 true] [17592186329154 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329154 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329154 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329158 13194139535062 true] [17592186329154 1110 #uuid "bccae9b9-8a13-5be0-a550-44f1ab868ce4" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329155 1034 "$2.5$ and $6.5$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329155 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329155 1111 #uuid "767c078a-cb2b-50a8-9863-f6a9ec44a99c" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329156 1034 "$0.5$ and $4.5$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329156 1111 #uuid "e16e11f9-5c15-508b-880c-49fd8092b8ac" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329157 1034 "$2$ and $6$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329157 1111 #uuid "dcd1d204-8c8d-54cd-9cae-7d3968da15bc" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329158 1034 "$1$ and $5$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329158 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329158 1111 #uuid "a4790855-b2ff-5fbd-89bc-bd5610e7584a" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329159 1034 "$1.5$ and $5.5$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329159 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329159 1111 #uuid "445238b3-cd2c-5c0b-9c79-d7b44681392c" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329160 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329161 13194139535062 true] [17592186329160 1089 "q-78512" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329160 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535062 true] [17592186329160 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/d6f30f0e475fc222dc54c4e7f04bc3f48442b8430c3b830bfd7d11a5d6d788e3.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y-2x=-11$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329160 1113 #uuid "8c7d9d94-79b2-54cd-80f6-31a00c58749c" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329160 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation $y-2x=-11,$ as follows: \n\n$$\ny=2x-11\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=2x-11$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x-11)^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+4{x}^{2}-44x+121 & =5 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-50x+130 & =5 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-50x+125 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+25 & =0 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-5 & =0 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-11 \\\\  & =2(5)-11 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y-2x=-11$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ at the point $(5,-1).$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329162 13194139535062 true] [17592186329161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329163 13194139535062 true] [17592186329161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329164 13194139535062 true] [17592186329161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329165 13194139535062 true] [17592186329161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329166 13194139535062 true] [17592186329161 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329161 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329161 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329165 13194139535062 true] [17592186329161 1110 #uuid "5d991269-4eb3-5eba-8f30-af287cdbf0ef" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329162 1034 "(5,-2)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329162 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329162 1111 #uuid "f65f1c7b-5b27-546f-988e-865db97af5c8" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329163 1034 "(4,-1)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329163 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329163 1111 #uuid "2a1ac0fd-55af-5889-9e71-309f5a2df18d" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329164 1034 "(6,-1)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329164 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329164 1111 #uuid "0fe3383f-d918-5607-81c5-ae7e512a51bc" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329165 1034 "(5,-1)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329165 1111 #uuid "b52026d1-8328-514b-90e9-7a1e871d6c3f" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329166 1034 "(6,-2)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535062 true] [17592186329166 1111 #uuid "21b7dd06-9298-5a34-8c2b-1b12c99bd369" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329167 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329168 13194139535062 true] [17592186329167 1089 "q-78651" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329167 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535062 true] [17592186329167 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ intersects the line $2x-y=-3.$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329167 1113 #uuid "4e85f65e-e9ff-5ffd-af20-284e643c5763" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329167 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation $2x-y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=2x+3\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=2x+3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(2x+3-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(2x+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+16+4{x}^{2}+4x+1 & =4 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+12x+13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={12}^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =144-260 \\\\  & =-116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-116<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $5{x}^{2}+12x+13=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle $(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ does not intersect the line $2x-y=-3$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/00bc159e9c25b913454c6037a822e41931c6224f3e6f0180da550fe9c857e718.png)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329169 13194139535062 true] [17592186329168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329170 13194139535062 true] [17592186329168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329171 13194139535062 true] [17592186329168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329172 13194139535062 true] [17592186329168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329173 13194139535062 true] [17592186329168 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329168 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329168 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329171 13194139535062 true] [17592186329168 1110 #uuid "62d5c5b0-c924-5b7f-8b0a-4854761b0596" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329169 1034 "$-4$ and $-2$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329169 1111 #uuid "1046217e-9437-5289-8209-b079a3ed3e21" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329170 1034 "$-4$ and $2$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329170 1111 #uuid "fc0c9670-1389-5aab-8bfb-da2fb7db4d95" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329171 1034 "The circle and the line do not intersect" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329171 1111 #uuid "b509a1e3-8b23-516a-853b-2cc4c5616ae0" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329172 1034 "$0$ and $4$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329172 1111 #uuid "5496e9de-7ea6-5674-ba15-be502b995ef3" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329173 1034 "$-1.5$ and $3$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329173 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329173 1111 #uuid "b999e9a8-1f8f-5099-a510-4b9555426023" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329174 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329175 13194139535062 true] [17592186329174 1089 "q-78654" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329174 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535062 true] [17592186329174 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2$ intersects the line $y=2x+1.$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329174 1113 #uuid "124f9d08-ccb9-500f-a740-588c701739e8" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329174 1145 "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=2x+1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x+1-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+4{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =2 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-10x+8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-10)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =100-160 \\\\  & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-60<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $5{x}^{2}-10x+8=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2$ does not intersect the line $y=2x+1.$ \n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/56e313584d7de2f1c40302135b00ef80a1d3c1e920407c9c4a0c62c3c6769456.png)" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329176 13194139535062 true] [17592186329175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329177 13194139535062 true] [17592186329175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329178 13194139535062 true] [17592186329175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329179 13194139535062 true] [17592186329175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329180 13194139535062 true] [17592186329175 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535062 true] [17592186329175 1041 "selection" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329175 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329179 13194139535062 true] [17592186329175 1110 #uuid "34bd55fe-b38d-5c67-92d4-c5e35befbbe1" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329176 1034 "$2$ and $4$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329176 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329176 1111 #uuid "e0cbed9a-76fc-5516-abfa-21e54e5f6073" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329177 1034 "$-1$ and $2$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329177 1111 #uuid "42923d41-651f-5905-b4c1-3103c3bbc634" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329178 1034 "$-1$ and $-2$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329178 1111 #uuid "32c1fbf7-f99c-513f-a695-fd26537f157c" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329179 1034 "The circle and the line do not intersect" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329179 1111 #uuid "a3df063b-9205-5f0e-b72b-66f776a24421" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329180 1034 "$3$ and $2$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535062 true] [17592186329180 1111 #uuid "c5cfe886-10e4-5718-a993-c95f75626d46" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329181 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329182 13194139535062 true] [17592186329181 1089 "q-78784" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329181 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535062 true] [17592186329181 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/36e373800d2bd917e38bec197fbb5add2ae785270c68ddb4daf740ad678d4ece.png)\n\nThe line $x+y=2$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, find the equation of the circle." 13194139535062 true] [17592186329181 1113 #uuid "6a7d9a2f-2a4c-5379-aee8-ef76e2496ac3" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329181 1145 "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & =2 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+y & =2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the  coordinates $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2+0}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(1,1).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(1,1)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(2,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{(1-2)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(1,1)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{2},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535062 true] [17592186329182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329183 13194139535062 true] [17592186329182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329184 13194139535062 true] [17592186329182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329185 13194139535062 true] [17592186329182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329186 13194139535062 true] [17592186329182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329187 13194139535062 true] [175921863